Found by the grep asked for in #10. Same class as #10, worse: the out-param
is not merely passed wrong, it is the wrong kind of object entirely.

t/34-cherrypick-revert.t:66-80 and 86-100

    my $cherryped_buf = "\0" x 20;            # git_oid-sized buffer
    my ($cherryped_ptr) = scalar_to_buffer($cherryped_buf);
    my $rc_cpc = Git::Libgit2::FFI::git_cherrypick_commit(
      $cherryped_ptr, $repo, $commit1, $commit1, 1, undef
    );
    ...
      my $cherryped_hex = oid_to_hex($cherryped_ptr);

Both functions are attached as
[ "opaque*", "git_repository", "git_commit", "git_commit", "uint", "opaque" ].
arg0 is `git_index **out`: the merged index, a handle that must be released
with git_index_free. It is not a git_oid, and neither function produces a
commit OID -- they produce the index that results from applying the change.

Three problems:

1. A raw address for an opaque* out-param means FFI::Platypus passes NULL
   (proven: git_libgit2_version, which does not NULL-check, segfaults when
   given a raw scalar_to_buffer address). The buffer is never written.
2. oid_to_hex($cherryped_ptr) in the rc==0 branch would hex-dump that
   untouched buffer -- 40 zeros -- and note it as a result.
3. Both branches assert unconditionally: ok(1, ...) on success,
   ok($rc != 0, ...) on failure. The pair passes for every possible rc.
   Today rc is -1 for both calls, so the success branch is dead code.

Same shape at t/34-cherrypick-revert.t:62: git_cherrypick itself returns
-13 (GIT_ECONFLICT) and is only note()d, never asserted.

Fixing needs a decision, not a ref swap: pick a scenario where cherrypick
and revert actually succeed (the current one applies commit1 onto itself),
take the index out-param with a scalar ref, assert something real about it
(git_index_entrycount / git_index_has_conflicts), and git_index_free it. If
the no-op case is worth keeping, assert the specific rc and the
git_error_last message instead of ok($rc != 0).

Whatever lands must be mutation-checked: change an expected value, see red,
change it back.
Fixed in t/34-cherrypick-revert.t (uncommitted, working tree). Scenario
rebuilt; both dead branches gone.

Decision: the old scenario (pick c1 onto c1) could not succeed, for two
independent reasons -- the picked commit had no parent to diff against, and
mainline was passed as 1 for a non-merge commit, which libgit2 rejects
outright. So the "no-op" framing was not a real case at all; it was an
argument error. Rebuilt as a conflict-free scenario and asserted success:

  c1 (a.txt)          base, refs/heads/main
  |\
  | c2 (a.txt b.txt)  refs/heads/topic
  c3 (a.txt c.txt)    refs/heads/main

- git_cherrypick_commit(\$cp_index, repo, c2, c3, 0, undef): rc 0, handle
  returned, git_index_entrycount == 3 (a/b/c, no conflict stages).
- the mainline misuse is kept, but as a real negative assertion: mainline=1
  on a non-merge commit -> rc != 0 plus Error->last->message =~ /not a merge
  commit/.
- git_revert_commit(\$rv_index, repo, c2, c2, 0, undef): rc 0, handle,
  entrycount == 1 (b.txt undone, a.txt left).
- git_cherrypick at the old :62 no longer hides GIT_ECONFLICT. The -13 was
  an artifact of the fixture, not of cherry-picking: the repository had
  commits but had never been checked out, so the safe checkout inside
  git_cherrypick collided with the missing files. The test now does
  git_checkout_options_init + git_checkout_head first and asserts rc == 0,
  b.txt in the working directory with "content b\n", and CHERRY_PICK_HEAD
  present.

Ownership: every git_index handle is freed (guarded with `if $handle`, so
the failure path frees nothing rather than freeing garbage); trees,
treebuilders, commits, signature and repository all freed as before. HEAD is
pinned to refs/heads/main right after init -- the old file did not do that
and git_cherrypick needs HEAD to resolve.

Note on the parent array: git_commit_create wants
`const git_commit *parents[]`, so the handle is pack("J", $commit1) and the
address of that buffer is passed. First place in t/ that creates a commit
with a parent.

Falsifiability proven by mutation, behaviour-side rather than
expectation-side (each on a scratch copy, run, reverted):
- pick c2 onto c2 instead of c3        -> not ok 4 (entrycount)
- mainline=1 on the success call       -> not ok 2,3,4
- mainline=0 on the misuse call        -> not ok 5,6
- revert c2 onto c3 instead of itself  -> not ok 9
- mainline=1 on revert_commit          -> not ok 7,8,9
- drop the git_checkout_head           -> not ok 10,11,12,13
- change the b.txt blob content        -> not ok 12

t/34-cherrypick-revert.t: 13 tests, pass. Full prove -lr t/: 26 files,
5244 tests, pass.

